      en: "Maskall Church in Belize is a historic Anglican church built in the 19th century by British settlers. Located in the village of Maskall in the Belize District, it is known for its modest wooden architecture typical of the colonial era. The church was consecrated in 1854 and served the local sugar plantation community. Today it is a protected cultural landmark and is used for worship and community events. The surrounding landscape is characterized by tropical vegetation, and the church exemplifies the cultural influence of British colonial rule in the region.",
